== hub->lesson consistency greps
7:  <meta name="description" content="How quantity responds to price, measured as a unit-free elasticity; how an equilibrium price moves when a demand parameter moves, by the implicit-function theorem; and how the finite-difference check of that derivative behaves as the step shrinks.">
38:          <li><strong>Uses:</strong> <a class="concept" data-concept="competitive-equilibrium" href="L03.html#def-competitive-equilibrium">competitive market equilibrium</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="derivative" href="../../../concepts.html#derivative">derivative</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="partial-derivative" href="../../../concepts.html#partial-derivative">partial derivative</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="implicit-function-theorem" href="../../../concepts.html#implicit-function-theorem">implicit-function theorem</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="taylor-theorem" href="../../../concepts.html#taylor-theorem">Taylor&rsquo;s theorem with remainder</a></li>
52:      <p class="why-it-matters">A demand curve answers two questions that one price cannot: how far quantity moves when price moves, and where the market settles when a condition changes. The first is measured by the price elasticity, defined in §1, a unit-free ratio of percent changes. The second is measured by comparative statics, defined in §2, the derivative of the equilibrium price with respect to a parameter. For linear demand the elasticity is \(-bp/(a-bp)\), and its size exceeds 1 exactly above the midpoint \(a/(2b)\). Lab 1 differentiates the equilibrium price of one market by the implicit-function theorem and checks that derivative against finite differences. Lesson L05 (its Definition 5.1, monopoly pricing) uses the elasticity of Definition 1.1.</p>
52:      <p class="why-it-matters">A demand curve answers two questions that one price cannot: how far quantity moves when price moves, and where the market settles when a condition changes. The first is measured by the price elasticity, defined in §1, a unit-free ratio of percent changes. The second is measured by comparative statics, defined in §2, the derivative of the equilibrium price with respect to a parameter. For linear demand the elasticity is \(-bp/(a-bp)\), and its size exceeds 1 exactly above the midpoint \(a/(2b)\). Lab 1 differentiates the equilibrium price of one market by the implicit-function theorem and checks that derivative against finite differences. Lesson L05 (its Definition 5.1, monopoly pricing) uses the elasticity of Definition 1.1.</p>
138:        <h3>Lab 1 · Demand-intercept shift: the implicit derivative against finite differences</h3>
7:  <meta name="description" content="A single seller facing linear demand and a constant marginal cost: the profit-maximising price by completing the square, the Lerner index equal to the inverse absolute elasticity at the optimum, and what the monopoly price does and does not imply.">
52:      <p class="why-it-matters">A seller that faces the whole market demand chooses its own price, and buyers respond along that demand curve. This lesson solves that choice for linear demand and a constant marginal cost. Monopoly pricing (Definition 5.1) sets a price above cost, and its markup, the Lerner index (Proposition 5.4), equals the inverse of the absolute price elasticity, \(1/|\varepsilon|\), from Lesson L04 at the chosen price. The profit is quadratic in price, so completing the square (Proposition 5.2) solves it. Lab 1 checks the closed form by a grid search in exact arithmetic, and checks the markup identity for three cases. Lesson L06 changes the setting to two sellers.</p>
38:          <li><strong>Uses:</strong> <a class="concept" data-concept="monopoly" href="L05.html#def-monopoly">monopoly pricing</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="cournot-competition" href="L06.html#def-cournot-competition">Cournot competition</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="bertrand-competition" href="L06.html#def-bertrand-competition">Bertrand competition</a>, <a class="concept" data-concept="definite-integral" href="../../../concepts.html#definite-integral">definite integral</a></li>
