== SIMULATED in project page
317:          <dd>Three whole numbers, \(a\), \(b\) and \(c\), with \(a > bc > 0\): \(a\) in trades at a fee of zero, \(b\) in trades per (cent per trade), and \(c\) in cents per trade. The autograder's instance comes from <code>grader/instance.py</code>, seeded by your workspace ID. It is SIMULATED: the generator draws \(b\) from 1 to 3, \(c\) from 5 to 60 and the gap \(d = a - bc\) from 60 to 400. None of it is exchange data. The memo uses the reference case, \(a = 120\), \(b = 1\), \(c = 20\), which is an input and not data.</dd>
== L04 Problem R2 mention of IFT in L02 line
367:            <p>Assuming the implicit-function theorem (Assumption 1 in the solution), and using only the first-order condition \(p = \mathrm{MC}(q^*)\) and the chain rule, show that \(d\pi^*/dp = q^*\) for \(p > c\).</p>
369:              <p><em>Assumption 1</em> (implicit-function theorem, an M2 result that this page states and does not prove): if \(G(q, p) = 0\) at \((q_0, p_0)\), \(G\) is continuously differentiable, and \(\partial G/\partial q \ne 0\) there, then near \((q_0, p_0)\) the equation defines \(q\) as a differentiable function of \(p\).</p>
488:        <p>This lesson cites no passage from a registry source. Each result on this page is proved on the page, or is checked by Lab 1 for the cases the lab lists, and the proof or the lab carries it. Problem R2 uses the implicit-function theorem, which Assumption 1 states and this page does not prove. The curriculum names Varian, <em>Intermediate Microeconomics</em> (registry key <code>varian2014intermediate</code>), as the source for this unit. No passage locator from it has been confirmed, so no statement here is attributed to it.</p>
